1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,430
Good morning, everyone.

2
00:00:01,430 --> 00:00:07,050
I've been invited to talk about my research project into Australian Aboriginal rock paintings.

3
00:00:07,050 --> 00:00:09,640
The Australian Aborigines have recorded both real

4
00:00:09,640 --> 00:00:14,820
and symbolic images of their time on rock walls for many thousands of years.

5
00:00:14,820 --> 00:00:18,180
Throughout the long history of this tradition

6
00:00:18,480 --> 00:00:21,770
new images have appeared and new painting styles have developed.

7
00:00:21,770 --> 00:00:27,060
And these characteristics can be used to categorise the different artistic styles.

8
00:00:27,060 --> 00:00:32,950
Among these are what we call the Dynamic, Yam and Modern styles of painting.

9
00:00:32,950 --> 00:00:40,550
One of the most significant characteristics of the different styles is the way that humans are depicted in the paintings.

10
00:00:40,550 --> 00:00:44,770
The more recent paintings show people in static poses.

11
00:00:44,770 --> 00:00:48,820
But the first human images to dominate rock art paintings

12
00:00:48,820 --> 00:00:52,170
over 8,000 years ago, were full of movement.

13
00:00:52,170 --> 00:00:54,078
These paintings showed people hunting

14
00:00:54,078 --> 00:00:59,800
and cooking food and so they were given the name 'Dynamic' to reflect this energy.

15
00:00:59,800 --> 00:01:04,800
It's quite amazing considering they were painted in such a simple stick-like form.

16
00:01:04,800 --> 00:01:11,110
In the Yam period, there was a movement away from stick figures to a more naturalistic shape.

17
00:01:11,110 --> 00:01:15,976
However, they didn't go as far as the Modern style, which is known as 'x-ray'

18
00:01:15,976 --> 00:01:22,140
because it actually makes a feature of the internal skeleton as well as the organs of animals and humans.

19
00:01:22,140 --> 00:01:30,663
The Yam style of painting got its name from the fact that it featured much curvier figures that actually resemble the vegetable called a yam,

20
00:01:30,663 --> 00:01:33,050
which is similar to a sweet potato.

21
00:01:33,050 --> 00:01:34,995
The Modern paintings are interesting

22
00:01:34,995 --> 00:01:40,440
because they include paintings at the time of the first contact with European settlers.

23
00:01:40,440 --> 00:01:44,440
Aborigines managed to convey the idea of the settlers

24
00:01:44,800 --> 00:01:47,299
clothing by simply painting the Europeans without any hands,

25
00:01:47,299 --> 00:01:51,490
indicating the habit of standing with their hands in their pockets!

26
00:01:51,490 --> 00:01:58,097
Size is another characteristic. The more recent images tend to be life size or even larger,

27
00:01:58,097 --> 00:02:01,400
but the Dynamic figures are painted in miniature.

28
00:02:01,400 --> 00:02:08,600
Aboriginal rock art also records the environmental changes that occurred over thousands of years.

29
00:02:08,600 --> 00:02:13,751
For example, we know from the Dynamic paintings that over 8,000 years ago,

30
00:02:13,751 --> 00:02:19,270
Aborigines would have rarely eaten fish and sea levels were much lower at this time.

31
00:02:19,270 --> 00:02:24,891
In fact, fish didn't start to appear in paintings until the Yam period along

32
00:02:24,891 --> 00:02:27,300
with shells and other marine images.

33
00:02:27,300 --> 00:02:30,979
The paintings of the Yam tradition also suggest that,

34
00:02:30,979 --> 00:02:36,703
during this time, the Aborigines moved away from animals as their main food source

35
00:02:36,703 --> 00:02:41,200
and began including vegetables in their diet, as these feature prominently.

36
00:02:41,200 --> 00:02:49,860
Freshwater creatures didn't appear in the paintings until the Modern period from 4,000 years ago.

37
00:02:49,860 --> 00:02:54,313
So, these paintings have already taught us a lot.

38
00:02:54,313 --> 00:03:01,240
But one image that has always intrigued us is known as the 'Rainbow Serpent'.

39
00:03:01,240 --> 00:03:05,502
The Rainbow Serpent, which is the focus of my most recent project,

40
00:03:05,502 --> 00:03:09,420
gets its name from its snake or serpent-like body

41
00:03:09,420 --> 00:03:13,670
and it first appeared in the Yam period 4 to 6,000 years ago.

42
00:03:13,670 --> 00:03:18,830
Many believe it is a curious mixture of kangaroo, snake and crocodile.

43
00:03:18,830 --> 00:03:22,985
But we decided to study the Rainbow Serpent paintings to see

44
00:03:23,025 --> 00:03:27,120
if we could locate the animal that the very first painters based their image on

45
00:03:27,120 --> 00:03:32,600
The Yam period coincided with the end of the last ice age.

46
00:03:32,600 --> 00:03:35,800
This brought about tremendous change in the environment,

47
00:03:35,800 --> 00:03:39,400
with the sea levels rising and creeping steadily inland.

48
00:03:39,400 --> 00:03:41,729
This flooded many familiar land features

49
00:03:41,729 --> 00:03:47,940
and also caused a great deal of disruption to traditional patterns of life, hunting in particular.

50
00:03:47,940 --> 00:03:54,030
New shores were formed and totally different creatures would have washed up onto the shores.

51
00:03:54,030 --> 00:03:57,126
We studied 107 paintings of the Rainbow Serpent

52
00:03:57,126 --> 00:04:02,931
and found that the one creature that matches it most closely was the Ribboned Pipefish,

53
00:04:02,931 --> 00:04:05,640
which is a type of sea horse.

54
00:04:05,640 --> 00:04:11,620
This sea creature would have been a totally unfamiliar sight in the inland regions

55
00:04:11,620 --> 00:04:12,781
where the image is found

56
00:04:12,781 --> 00:04:16,540
and may have been the inspiration behind the early paintings.

57
00:04:16,540 --> 00:04:22,600
So, at the end of the ice age there would have been enormous changes in animal and plant life.

58
00:04:22,600 --> 00:04:29,350
It's not surprising then, that the Aborigines linked this abundance to the new creatures they witnessed.

59
00:04:29,350 --> 00:04:34,120
Even today, Aborigines see the Rainbow Serpent as a symbol of creation,

60
00:04:34,120 --> 00:04:37,300
which is understandable given the increase in vegetation

61
00:04:37,300 --> 00:04:42,070
and the new life forms that featured when the image first appeared.
